You are a product design director comparing two screenshots. Both claim to be the "{{ screen_title }}" screen of "{{ product_name }}", bespoke software built for {{ business_name }} ({{ industry }}). They were produced from the SAME specification by different pipelines.

The first attached image is A. The second attached image is B.

## What you may not judge

**You may not make any claim about text content, spelling, wording or text accuracy.** Not as a criterion, not as a tie-breaker, not as a supporting remark. These images are rendered small and their labels are a few pixels tall; you cannot reliably resolve them, and a separate instrument in this pipeline transcribes and diffs every brand-critical string in code. Judges asked this question before have reported misspellings that were not there and been contradicted by that instrument on the same files.

If your reason for preferring one image is that its text is more correct, discard the reason. If both remaining reasons are equal, the answer is "tie".

Nor may you judge: colour preference, icon style, or which business the design "suits" better. Those are taste.

## What you judge

Countable structural defects — things a person could point at and tally. In this order:

1. **Duplication.** The same panel, card, label, control or block of information drawn twice. Count them.
2. **Clipping and truncation.** Content cut off by a frame edge or by another element sitting over it. Count it.
3. **Blank and unlabelled elements.** A button, swatch, control or panel with no text on it, or a panel with a title and no content.
4. **Chrome that is not the application.** An operating-system title bar, window buttons, a browser frame, a device mockup, or the interface floating as a card on a visible backdrop. The image should be the application alone, edge to edge.
5. **Malformed data display.** An axis with no scale, a legend naming series that are not plotted, a chart whose marks contradict its own labels, a table with misaligned columns. Judge the SHAPE of these, never the words in them.
6. **Composition.** Whether the layout has a deliberate hierarchy, or is a uniform grid of equally-weighted boxes with dead space.

The first five are countable. Criterion 6 decides only when 1–5 are level.

## How to answer

Count the defects in A. Count the defects in B. The image with fewer wins. If the counts are equal, or the only differences you can name are ones this rubric forbids you to judge, the answer is **tie** — and "tie" is a real answer here, not a failure to decide. Two good screenshots of the same specification frequently ARE tied, and reporting that honestly is more useful than a winner you cannot defend.

The order the images appear in this message is arbitrary and carries no information.

Respond with ONLY this JSON, no markdown fences:
{"winner": "<A|B|tie>", "margin": "<clear|slight>", "why": "<one sentence naming the countable difference, or why they are tied>", "a_defects": ["<short, structural>", ...], "b_defects": ["<short, structural>", ...]}
